`buildSecurityFactManifest` has three `for…of` loops (over `secretHits`, `depVulnFindings`, `sarifFindings`) that each `await sha256HexPrefix(…)` per iteration. Every hash is independent — no iteration depends on the previous hash — so the loops serialize work that could be issued concurrently. Replace each loop with `Promise.all(items.map(async item => { … }))` and collect the results into the manifest afterward. Example for the secret-hits loop:

```ts
const secretEntries = await Promise.all(args.secretHits.map(async h => {
  const hash = await sha256HexPrefix(`secret|${h.ruleId}|${h.path}|${h.line}|${h.redactedPreview}`, CONTENT_HASH_HEX)
  const evidenceId = `secret:${h.ruleId}:${hash}:${h.line}`
  return [evidenceId, { evidenceId, tool: "secret", path: h.path, line: h.line, cwe: SECRET_CWE, summary: normalizeSummary(…) }] as const
}))
for (const [id, fact] of secretEntries) manifest.set(id, fact)
```

Apply the same pattern to the dep-vuln and SARIF loops. Under typical loads (0–30 total facts) the wall-clock saving is modest (~10–50ms), but the fix is trivial and eliminates unnecessary sequential microtask yields on the orchestrator's critical pre-fan-out path.

The `containsIdentifier` function instantiates a new `RegExp` object on every call. It is invoked:

1. In `buildRetrievalRefs` (line ~310) once per retrieval chunk to rank by symbol mention — O(chunks) per card.
2. In `chunkExcerpt` (line ~335) once per line until match when centering excerpts.

